course: dictionary
lesson: 10451
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives several meanings for truss, so the context tells you which one is right. Ask yourself: is this word a thing, or an action? Is it a name, with a capital letter?
explain: «Liz Truss's government announced big tax cuts.» Here Truss is a person's surname, because it has a capital letter in the middle of a sentence and it follows a name. The word truss is not being used at all.
explain: A truss can be a thing: a frame of beams joined in triangles that holds up a roof or a bridge. A truss can also be an action: to truss something means to tie it up tightly. Read the words around it to decide which meaning fits.
fact: A truss is a frame of beams joined in triangles that holds up a roof or a bridge.
fact: To truss means to tie someone or something up tightly, such as a chicken before it is cooked.
fact: A truss is also an old-fashioned padded belt worn to support the body after an injury.
fact: A truss is a bunch of flowers or fruit on one stalk, like truss tomatoes.
fact: Triangles are strong shapes, which is why trusses in bridges and roofs are built from them. Capital letters can show a name, as in «Liz Truss».
exercise: «Liz Truss's government announced big tax cuts.» Why does Truss start with a capital letter? | *It is a person's name | It means to tie up tightly | It is the first word of the sentence | It means a frame of beams
exercise: The cook trussed the chicken before roasting it. What does trussed mean? | Cooked in a hot pan | Placed on a plate | *Tied up tightly | Cut into small pieces
exercise: Trusses in bridges are strong because they are built from which shape? | Squares only | Stars | *Triangles | Circles
test: In the phrase «truss tomatoes», what does truss mean? | A bridge built with triangles | A padded belt worn after an injury | *A bunch of flowers or fruit on one stalk | Tie up tightly
test: «The villain trussed up the hero with rope.» What does trussed up mean? | Lifted high into the air | *Tied up tightly | Painted bright colours | Set free and cheered
exam: «The steel truss held up the bridge, and the villain trussed up the hero.» In the second sentence, what does trussed up mean? | Hidden under a blanket | Cheered loudly | Given a warm drink | *Tied up tightly
